 ## Overview
-The Riley et al. Angular Distribution Renderer is a single-pass volume rendering method designed to approximate multiple scattering effects in participating media, specifically targeting the forward-scattering behavior of large atmospheric particles like water and ice [S2]. It solves the computational cost of integrating the in-scattering term over the sphere of directions by using pre-computed angular distribution functions to direct light into the eye ray, avoiding the premature darkening artifacts common in low-albedo models [S1]. Unlike [3D-Filter / Concrete Directions](12253-3d-filter-concrete-directions.md) which discretizes directions, this method uses a continuous angular distribution approximation within a half-angle slicing framework [S2].
+The Riley et al. Angular Distribution Renderer is a single-pass volume rendering method designed to approximate multiple scattering in participating media, specifically targeting large atmospheric particles like water and ice [S2]. It solves the computational complexity of the radiative transfer equation by precomputing angular distribution functions that describe how light spreads after a fixed number of scattering events, allowing the renderer to direct light into the eye ray in a single pass [S1, S2]. Unlike recursive ray casting or low-albedo approximations, this method captures essential single-scattering effects and chromatic phenomena (rainbows, halos) while avoiding the premature darkening artifacts common in simpler models [S1, S2].
 
 ## How it works
